<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fa Ngum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1316">1316</a>–c. 1393</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fa Ngum was the founder of Lan Xang, the first unified Lao kingdom. A prince of the Muang Sua ruling family raised in exile at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, he led an army up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> with Khmer support and took Muang Sua in 1353. He is credited with spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, and his nobles deposed him around 1373.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fa-ngum.jpg" alt="Fa Ngum" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Carr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fangum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
